From cab5b98fe04117dcfe834f761f164b49e91dbc2b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ANiri anland audio fix <anland-audio@localhost>
Date: Wed, 30 Sep 2026 00:41:32 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] backend/anland: fix playback queue framing, ownership and
 gating

Review follow-up to 4d17eff. Four defects, all in the sender-thread design
introduced there.

1. The queue slot was [slot.len][slot.epoch][audio_msg][PCM] but sender_send_one()
   handed the whole slot to sendmsg(), so the ring's local 16-byte header was
   transmitted as part of the protocol message. send_ring_peek() now reports the
   wire span separately: wire_off = slot start + sizeof(slot), wire_len =
   slot.len - sizeof(slot), and only [audio_msg][PCM] goes on the wire. The
   short-write check compares against wire_len, while send_ring_consume() still
   consumes the full slot_len so the parser stays aligned. The slot layout is
   documented in the struct.

2. sender_send_one() returned 0 both for "sent one" and for "queue empty", so
   sender_thread()'s `while (sender_send_one(...) == 0)` spun forever on an empty
   queue, and the same pattern in the exit flush made pthread_join() unable to
   return. Replaced with explicit SEND_EMPTY / SEND_SENT / SEND_AGAIN / SEND_DEAD.
   The flush is gone entirely: a detach must not deliver one last period to a
   consumer that is leaving, and waiting on the kernel there only extended the
   join. Whatever is queued at detach is dropped and the queue indices are reset,
   which is also safe because the producer is disarmed first.

3. audio_fd and sock_fd were the same duplicate, so the io source (created with
   close=true) and the sender thread both considered themselves owners of one
   descriptor. Now two independent F_DUPFD_CLOEXEC duplicates are taken: the io
   source owns io_fd and closes it through pw_loop_destroy_source(), the sender
   thread owns sender_fd and closes it itself, and the loop thread only closes
   sender_fd if the thread never started. Neither close can hit the other's
   descriptor.

4. Real PCM had no attachment gate: after a detach the RT callback still queued
   captured audio, so a newly attached consumer could receive PCM before it had
   announced anything. Added an atomic playback_ready that is set only when a
   PLAYBACK format is accepted on the current attachment and cleared on detach;
   both the real PCM path and the silence path are gated on it. Each queued slot
   is additionally tagged with the attachment epoch, and the sender discards any
   slot whose epoch is not current, so detach, reconnect and format changes cannot
   leak stale PCM into a new consumer even if a period was produced mid-detach.

Unchanged by request: the RT callback still never touches a socket, the mic
stream still does not use PW_STREAM_FLAG_RT_PROCESS, the mic ring remains
loop-thread-only, the SOCK_SEQPACKET framing checks (exact header/payload
agreement, MSG_TRUNC, whole mic frames) are untouched, and the keep-alive
behaviour is preserved. No other Niri code is touched.
---
 src/backend/anland/c/anland_audio.c | 292 ++++++++++++++++++----------
 1 file changed, 189 insertions(+), 103 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/backend/anland/c/anland_audio.c b/src/backend/anland/c/anland_audio.c
